statement.paragraph:
$A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 5 \\
      3 & 1 \\
      {- 2} & 4
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\textbf{b} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      {- 2} \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$

Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      3 & 4 \\
      {- 2} & 1 \\
      3 & 4
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\textbf{b} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      11 \\
      {- 9} \\
      5
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      5 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\mathbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      5 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Compute $A\mathbf{u}$ and $A\mathbf{v}$, and compare them with $\textbf{b}$. Could $\mathbf{u}$ possibly be a least-squares solution of $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$? (Answer this without computing a least-squares solution.)

Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      2 & 1 \\
      {- 3} & {- 4} \\
      3 & 2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\textbf{b} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      5 \\
      4 \\
      4
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\mathbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      6 \\
      {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Compute $A\mathbf{u}$ and $A\mathbf{v}$, and compare them with $\textbf{b}$. Is it possible that at least one of $\mathbf{u}$ or $\mathbf{v}$ could be a least-squares solution of $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$? (Answer this without computing a least-squares solution.)

$A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      2 & 3 \\
      2 & 4 \\
      1 & 1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      {2\text{/}3} & {- 1\text{/}3} \\
      {2\text{/}3} & {2\text{/}3} \\
      {1\text{/}3} & {- 2\text{/}3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      3 & 5 \\
      0 & 1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\textbf{b} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      7 \\
      3 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$
